// Title: NET NGenAssemblyUsageLog Registry Key Tamper
// Sigma ID: 28036918-04d3-423d-91c0-55ecf99fb892
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: frack113
// Date: 2022-11-18 | Modified: 2023-08-17
// ATT&CK: persistence, defense-impairment, T1112
// Description: Detects changes to the NGenAssemblyUsageLog registry key. .NET Usage Log output location can be
//   controlled by setting the NGenAssemblyUsageLog CLR configuration knob in the Registry or by
//   configuring an environment variable (as described in the next section). By simplify specifying
//   an arbitrary value (e.g. fake output location or junk data) for the expected value, a Usage Log
//   file for the .NET execution context will not be created.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://bohops.com/2021/03/16/investigating-net-clr-usage-log-tampering-techniques-for-edr-evasion/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_net_cli_ngenassemblyusagelog.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where RegistryKey endswith "SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\.NETFramework\\NGenAssemblyUsageLog"
